---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const idx = projects.findIndex((p) => p.slug === "atlassian");
const project = projects[idx];
const next = projects[(idx + 1) % projects.length];
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL; // "/portfolio-presence/full-time/"

const tldr = [
  { k: "Space", v: "Enterprise ITSM, mobile" },
  { k: "Product", v: "Mobile on-call scheduling, inside a company-level platform consolidation" },
  { k: "Method", v: "Task-based remote moderated usability (90 min) with SEQ, on a prototype" },
  { k: "Sample", v: "11 analysed of 12 recruited — 6 on-call engineers, 5 engineering & ops managers" },
  { k: "Partnered with", v: "Product, design, and engineering (triad)" },
  { k: "Outcome", v: "Mobile on-call priorities reframed; 4 design changes shipped, 2 roadmap additions" },
];
---
<Layout
  title="On-call scheduling research — Anmol Bhargava"
  description="Evaluative research on mobile on-call scheduling in enterprise ITSM that shifted the team's question from making scheduling faster to investing in the parts of the on-call workflow mobile actually serves."
>
  <article class="section section--cream">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 820px;">
      <a href={`${base}#work`} class="back-link">← Selected work</a>
      <div class="case-meta">Project {project.num} · {project.meta}</div>
      <h1 class="display display--xl" style="color: var(--brick);">{project.title}</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: 1rem;">{project.subtitle}</p>
      <div class="case-tags">
        {project.tags.map((t) => <span class="tag">{t}</span>)}
      </div>

      <!-- TL;DR -->
      <div class="tldr">
        <div class="tldr__label">TL;DR</div>
        <div class="tldr__grid">
          {tldr.map((item) => (
            <div>
              <div class="tldr__k">{item.k}</div>
              <div class="tldr__v">{item.v}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="tldr__shift">
          <div class="tldr__k">Strategic shift</div>
          <div class="tldr__v">
            “How do we make scheduling faster on mobile?” → “Which parts of the on-call workflow
            does mobile actually carry?”
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Problem -->
      <div class="section-label">The problem</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          An on-call engineer gets paged at 2am. Whether that moment goes well depends on
          unglamorous things: knowing you're actually on shift, an override that took effect, a
          schedule that hasn't quietly changed. In enterprise incident management, ambiguity is
          operational risk.
        </p>
        <p>
          The company was consolidating a standalone incident-management product into its flagship
          ITSM platform — an OKR-level bet, with mobile as part of it. New on-call scheduling
          features were headed for the mobile app, on the logic that faster, easier scheduling
          would help drive adoption.
        </p>
        <p>
          Inside that logic sat an assumption worth naming: that scheduling was what mobile
          on-call users needed most.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- The question underneath -->
      <div class="section-label">The question underneath</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Designs were in flight, so the evaluative question was urgent. But prior research argued
          against stopping there: earlier studies showed most users opened the mobile app to
          acknowledge and close alerts — not to browse schedules. So the study carried two
          questions at once.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The evaluative question</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Can engineers and managers find, understand, and operate the new scheduling components?
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The strategic question</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Is scheduling actually where mobile earns its place in the on-call workflow?
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- How it was run -->
      <div class="section-label">How it was run</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A deliberately tactical method, run strictly, matched to decision speed. Two cohorts
          mirrored the workflow's two actors — engineers, who live their own schedule, and
          managers, who are accountable for the team's — because in enterprise scheduling those
          are different jobs, not different demographics.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>Stakeholder interviews with design and PM before writing the discussion guide</span></li>
        <li><span>Screener and prototype piloted with internal on-call engineers</span></li>
        <li><span>Sessions observed live by the triad, tagged in Dovetail as they happened</span></li>
        <li><span>One participant excluded when their session contradicted their screener — 11 of 12 analysed</span></li>
        <li><span>Journey mapping declared out of scope up front; claims stayed inside the tested boundary</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Findings -->
      <div class="section-label">What we found</div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Engineers kept drifting toward alerts</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Asked to check their day, four of six engineers went to the alerts tab and called it the
          most useful part of the app. Schedule admin is occasional; alerts are daily.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">The workflow runs on predictability, not speed</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          The one feature built for speed — taking a shift “for an hour” in one tap — met shrugs:
          schedules are set well in advance. Users wanted to see weeks ahead, not save seconds.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">Learned patterns beat novel ones</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Participants wanted to keep colour associations they'd already built, and succeeded on
          the override paths that matched expectations while the novel one confused. In a
          six-or-seven-tool ecosystem, consistency outperforms cleverness.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="insight">
        <div class="insight__lead">One screen, two different jobs</div>
        <p class="insight__body">
          Managers called the “my schedules” framing noise — they never take call. What they
          reached for was whole-team visibility: coverage, gaps, who can fill a slot.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="verbatim">
          <p>
            “As a manager, I am never gonna fill in for anybody's absence. I am not gonna sit and
            take incident calls.”
          </p>
          <div class="verbatim__attr">— P7, engineering manager</div>
        </blockquote>
      </div>

      <!-- The key insight -->
      <div class="kx">
        <div class="kx__kicker">The key insight</div>
        <div class="kx__eq">
          <span class="kx__term">Predictability + learnability</span>
          <span class="kx__gt">&gt;</span>
          <span class="kx__term kx__term--dim">simply reducing task time</span>
        </div>
        <p class="kx__body">
          Mobile's value wasn't finishing scheduling tasks faster — it was knowing what's coming
          and trusting patterns already learned. A read across converging signals, not a measured
          effect; but every signal pointed this way, and none pointed toward speed.
        </p>
        <ul class="kx__ev">
          <li>Schedules set well in advance — the one-tap speed feature met shrugs</li>
          <li>Users asked to see weeks ahead, here and in prior studies</li>
          <li>Colour used as memory for people and shifts</li>
          <li>Familiar override paths succeeded; the novel one confused</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- What changed -->
      <div class="section-label">What changed</div>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><span>The team's question reframed: from “make scheduling faster” to “invest where mobile carries the workflow” — overrides, alert awareness, team visibility</span></li>
        <li><span>Manager view recommended to be rebuilt around team visibility, not personal schedules</span></li>
        <li><span>Card-view customisation deprioritised — the weakest task, and participants said so themselves</span></li>
        <li><span>4 design changes shipped, simplifying navigation and interaction patterns; 2 features added to the roadmap</span></li>
        <li><span>+15% satisfaction and ~+10% adoption post-launch — the team's post-launch measures, not this study's claim</span></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Stakeholders -->
      <div class="section-label">How I worked with the team</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A triad throughout — researcher, designer, PM, with engineering close. Insights were
          soft-shared and sparred on before the readout, then packaged as verbatims, Loom clips,
          and how-might-we prompts so design could act on them directly. Decisions stayed with
          product; research's job was making sure they were made against the workflow users
          actually have, not the one the roadmap assumed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Why it matters -->
      <div class="section-label">Why this matters in enterprise</div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Enterprise products are workflows before they are screens: multiple actors, tool
          ecosystems, competing priorities. A feature request is rarely the underlying problem —
          and research earns its keep by checking the team is solving the right one while there's
          still time to redirect.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Takeaway -->
      <div class="pullquote">
        <div class="pullquote__label">Takeaway</div>
        <p class="pullquote__text">
          Speed is easy to measure, which makes it easy to over-value. This workflow ran on
          predictability and learned patterns — and research's job was to notice which problem
          deserved the screen.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href={`${base}projects/${next.slug}`} class="btn btn--filled-brick">
          Next: {next.title.length > 40 ? next.title.slice(0, 40) + "…" : next.title} →
        </a>
        <a href={`${base}#contact`} class="btn">Get in touch</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .verbatim {
    margin: 1rem 0 0.25rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--cream-soft);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--brick);
    border-radius: 0 12px 12px 0;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .verbatim p {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .verbatim__attr {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .kx {
    background: var(--deep);
    color: var(--on-brick);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding: 2.5rem 2.25rem;
    margin: 3.5rem 0;
  }
  .kx__kicker {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    opacity: 0.7;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .kx__eq {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6rem 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .kx__term {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .kx__term--dim {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }
  .kx__gt {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 2.2rem);
    color: var(--peach);
  }
  .kx__body {
    font-size: 0.96rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    opacity: 0.92;
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }
  .kx__ev {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .kx__ev {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
      gap: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    }
  }
  .kx__ev li {
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
    opacity: 0.85;
  }
  .kx__ev li::before {
    content: "→";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--peach);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
